## 1. What is a Transformer?

A **Transformer** is a neural network architecture introduced in the 2017 paper **"Attention Is All You Need"**. It is designed to work with sequences such as text, and it is the foundation of almost all modern language models (GPT, Claude, Gemini, Llama, BERT, and more).

Its key idea is **self-attention**: instead of reading words one by one, the model looks at all words at once and decides which words are most relevant to each other.

**Example:**

> "The animal didn't cross the street because **it** was tired."

Attention helps the model work out that "it" refers to "animal", not "street".

---

## 2. Why Transformers Were Invented

Before Transformers, sequence models were mainly **RNNs** and **LSTMs**.

| Problem with RNN/LSTM | How Transformers fix it |
|---|---|
| Read words one at a time (slow, cannot parallelize) | Process all words at once (fast on GPUs) |
| Forget early words in long sentences | Attention connects any two words directly |
| Hard to scale to huge datasets | Scale very well with more data and compute |

---

## 3. Core Building Blocks

| Component | What it does |
|---|---|
| **Tokenization** | Splits text into tokens (words or sub-words) and converts them to numeric IDs |
| **Embeddings** | Converts token IDs into vectors that capture meaning |
| **Positional encoding** | Adds word-order information (the model sees all words at once, so it needs this) |
| **Self-attention** | Lets each word look at the other words in the same sentence to gain context |
| **Multi-head attention** | Runs several attention operations in parallel, each learning different relationships |
| **Cross-attention** | Lets the decoder look at the encoder's output |
| **Masked attention** | Stops the decoder from seeing future words while generating |
| **Feed-forward network** | Further transforms each token's representation |
| **Residual connections** | Shortcuts that help gradients flow and make deep models trainable |
| **Layer normalization** | Keeps values stable during training |
| **Output layer (Linear + Softmax)** | Turns the final vector into probabilities over the vocabulary |

