## 7. Glossary

| Term | Meaning |
|---|---|
| **Token** | A small piece of text (word, sub-word, or character) the model reads |
| **Vocabulary** | The full set of tokens a model knows |
| **Embedding** | A list of numbers (vector) representing a token's meaning |
| **Attention** | A mechanism for weighing how relevant each token is to another |
| **Query, Key, Value (Q, K, V)** | Three vectors used in attention: the query asks, keys are matched against it, values are what gets passed on |
| **Context window** | Maximum number of tokens the model can consider at once |
| **Parameters** | The learned numbers (weights) inside the model |
| **Loss** | A score of how wrong the model's predictions are; training tries to reduce it |
| **Perplexity** | A measure of how well a language model predicts text (lower is better) |
| **Epoch** | One full pass through the training data |
| **Batch** | A group of training samples processed together |
| **Overfitting** | Model memorizes training data and performs badly on new data |
| **Pretraining** | Initial large-scale training on general text |
| **Fine-tuning** | Further training on smaller, specific data |
| **RLHF** | Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback, used to align models |
| **DPO** | Direct Preference Optimization, a simpler alternative to RLHF |
| **Quantization** | Using lower-precision numbers to make models smaller and faster |
| **Inference** | Using a trained model to produce outputs |
| **Hallucination** | When a model confidently produces false information |

