## 8. FAQ (Common Doubts)

**Q1. What is the difference between an encoder and a decoder?**
The encoder *understands* the input and turns it into meaningful vectors. The decoder *generates* output, one token at a time, using those vectors and what it has already generated.

**Q2. Why does the decoder generate one word at a time?**
Each word depends on the previous words. The model predicts the next token, adds it to the sequence, and repeats. This is called autoregressive generation.

**Q3. What is self-attention in simple words?**
Every word asks: "Which other words in this sentence help me understand my meaning?" and gives more weight to the relevant ones.

**Q4. Why do we need positional encoding?**
The Transformer processes all tokens at the same time, so it has no natural sense of order. Positional encoding tells it that "dog bites man" is different from "man bites dog".

**Q5. What is the difference between self-attention and cross-attention?**
Self-attention looks at tokens within the same sequence. Cross-attention lets the decoder look at the encoder's output (the source sentence).

**Q6. What is masked attention?**
When generating, the model must not see future words. A mask hides them so predictions depend only on earlier tokens.

**Q7. What is multi-head attention?**
Several attention operations run in parallel, each focusing on different relationships (for example grammar, meaning, or long-distance links). Their results are combined.

**Q8. What is the difference between pretraining and fine-tuning?**
Pretraining teaches general language on huge data. Fine-tuning adapts the model to follow instructions or handle a specific task using much smaller data.

**Q9. Can I train an LLM on my own laptop?**
You can train a *small* model (a few million parameters) for learning purposes, for example a tiny GPT on a small dataset. Training a large, production-quality LLM needs many GPUs and a large budget. A practical alternative is to fine-tune an existing open model using techniques like LoRA.

**Q10. What is the difference between an LLM and a chatbot?**
An LLM is the underlying model. A chatbot is an application built on top of it, usually using a fine-tuned, aligned version plus a chat interface.

**Q11. What is the difference between a base model and an instruct/chat model?**
A base model just continues text. An instruct/chat model has been fine-tuned to follow instructions and hold conversations.

**Q12. Why do LLMs hallucinate?**
They are trained to predict likely next tokens, not to verify facts. When unsure, they can produce fluent but incorrect text. Techniques like retrieval (RAG), better training data, and alignment reduce this but do not eliminate it.

**Q13. What is a context window?**
The maximum amount of text (in tokens) the model can take into account at once. Longer context lets it handle bigger documents.

**Q14. What is RAG?**
Retrieval-Augmented Generation: the system searches a knowledge source for relevant documents and gives them to the LLM along with the question, so answers are grounded in real data.

**Q15. Do I need to learn math?**
Basic linear algebra (vectors, matrices), probability, and calculus concepts (gradients) help a lot, but you can start building and learning by coding first.

